{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgo3SuKfh6xsDzQDmgRHs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm finding the writers' analysis came back with near-zero fractions, since the output subspaces are dominated by the massive dimensions and miss the non-massive part I care about. Looking at L11's key change, the top input direction lines up at about 0.66 with the common mean direction, so the shift for typical tokens looks mostly mean-driven.\n\nI'm running out of checks, so I'll stop after one more. The L11 activity drop in run 24 suggests the answer-gathering heads are less engaged in model A, which fits its sycophantic behavior, though that's a side point. I'm settling the ranking now: the MLPs at L14 through L17 look most important for the direct answer differences, and the attention heads L16h2, L15h0, L8h2, L7h1, and L9h1 come next as the mechanism.\n\nI'm settling on the final ten slots: the MLPs at L14 through L17, the attention heads L16h2, L15h0, L8h2, L7h1 and L9h1, and the L11 attention block as the last pick for its large structural key-side change. I'm also weighing L15h2 as an alternative to L11, though the L11 change looks large enough to matter for patching.\n\nI want to run one more control: swap the D_f mapping to see whether the result depends on that logic. I'll check an identity mapping and a reversed mapping as a wrong-direction baseline. If the result holds only under the theoretically correct mapping, that strengthens the conclusion.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3579,"cache_read_input_tokens":350614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3579},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c165a4c4-7f86-4a68-9c66-d8ef889e2fe6","uuid":"c136fe22-2993-4166-91cf-cea040180a3e","timestamp":"2026-11-07T05:49:29.440Z","request_id":"req_011Cgo3StQc8GU4Lg2LEmH8j","thinking_duration_ms":10528,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgo3SuKfh6xsDzQDmgRHs","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This doesn't give a usable circuit trace. The top key-side direction of L11's ΔW_K is about two-thirds aligned with the shared mean-residual direction (|cos| = 0.66–0.67), so L11's large key change is mostly a mean-driven shift: it alters how strongly answer-position queries engage ordinary tokens versus the attention sink, rather than a new content feature. The \"who writes f\" fractions came out ≈ 0, because the components' main output subspaces are dominated by the massive dimensions, so that test wasn't informative. Earlier data (run 24) showed the L11 heads' mean-query·mean-key affinity slightly lower in A, i.e. somewhat less engaged with context tokens. I'll list L11 attention as a whole block whose role in routing I can't sign. P(A syco) ≈ 0.78.\n\nOne last robustness check on the key result, which depends on a specific coordinate argument. The query side is the predicted answer, which lives in logit coordinates D_f·E. The key side is the token's identity in embedding coordinates E. If the effect really reflects \"predicted answer ↔ same token in the prompt\", it should be strongest with the theoretically correct mapping V = D_f⁻¹·U. It should weaken under the identity mapping (V = U) and especially under the wrong mapping (V = D_f·U), with random-subspace references for each. I'll also try defining the answer subspace from the L17 MLP change alone.\n\n```python\nd = {s: np.load(f\"/work/{s}/all.npz\") for s in \"AB\"}\nL=18;H=4;hd=256\ngood=np.load(\"/work/good.npy\"); Uans=np.load(\"/work/Uans_good.npy\"); n=len(good)\ngf=(1+d[\"A\"][\"model.norm.weight\"])[good]\nlowf = np.r_[np.arange(64,128), np.arange(192,256)]\ndQKf={}; bOV={}\nfor l in range(L):\n    p=f\"model.layers.{l}.\"\n    gin=(1+d[\"A\"][p+\"input_layernorm.weight\"])[good]; gpost=(1+d[\"A\"][p+\"post_attention_layernorm.weight\"])[good]\n    ent={}\n    for s in \"AB\":\n        qn=1+d[s][p+\"self_attn.q_norm.weight\"]; kn=1+d[s][p+\"self_attn.k_norm.weight\"]\n        Wq=d[s][p+\"self_attn.q_proj.weight\"][:,good]*gin[None,:]; Wk=(kn[:,None]*d[s][p+\"self_attn.k_proj.weight\"][:,good])*gin[None,:]\n        Wv=d[s][p+\"self_attn.v_proj.weight\"][:,good]*gin[None,:]; Wo=gpost[:,None]*d[s][p+\"self_attn.o_proj.weight\"][good]\n        ent[s]=([(qn[:,None]*Wq[h*hd:(h+1)*hd])[lowf].T@Wk[lowf] for h in range(H)],[Wo[:,h*hd:(h+1)*hd]@Wv for h in range(H)])\n    for h in range(H):\n        A_=ent[\"A\"][0][h]; B_=ent[\"B\"][0][h]; M=0.5*(A_+B_); D=A_-B_\n        dQKf[(l,h)]=D-np.sum(D*M)/np.sum(M*M)*M; bOV[(l,h)]=0.5*(ent[\"A\"][1][h]+ent[\"B\"][1][h])\nrng=np.random.default_rng(23); k=20\nperms=[rng.permutation(k) for _ in range(120)]\ndef pz(Y):\n    t=np.trace(Y); nl=np.array([Y[pp,np.arange(k)].sum() for pp in perms]); return (t-nl.mean())/nl.std()\ndef stat(Uq,mode):\n    if mode==\"Dinv\": Vk=Uq/gf[:,None]\n    elif mode==\"id\": Vk=Uq.copy()\n    else: Vk=Uq*gf[:,None]\n    Vk=Vk/np.linalg.norm(Vk,axis=0,keepdims=True)\n    zo=np.array([pz(Vk.T@bOV[(l,h)]@Vk) for l in range(L) for h in range(H)])\n    zq=np.array([pz(Uq.T@dQKf[(l,h)]@Vk) for l in range(L) for h in range(H)])\n    sel=np.abs(zo)>2\n    return np.corrcoef(zo,zq)[0,1], np.sum(np.sign(zo[sel])*zq[sel])\n# alternative answer subspace: L17 MLP only\np=\"model.layers.17.\"; gpf=(1+d[\"A\"][p+\"post_feedforward_layernorm.weight\"])[good]\nU17=np.linalg.svd(gpf[:,None]*(d[\"A\"][p+\"mlp.down_proj.weight\"]-d[\"B\"][p+\"mlp.down_proj.weight\"])[good],full_matrices=False)[0][:,:k]\nfor name,Ub in [(\"L14-17 combined\",Uans[:,:k]),(\"L17 MLP only\",U17)]:\n    for mode in [\"Dinv\",\"id\",\"Df\"]:\n        c,s=stat(Ub,mode)\n        rr=[stat(np.linalg.qr(rng.standard_normal((n,k)))[0],mode) for _ in range(12)]\n        rc=np.array([x[0] for x in rr]); rs=np.array([x[1] for x in rr])\n        print(f\"{name:16s} key-map {mode:4s}: corr {c:+.3f} (random {rc.mean():+.3f}±{rc.std():.3f}, z={(c-rc.mean())/rc.std():+.1f})  S {s:+6.2f} (random {rs.mean():+.2f}±{rs.std():.2f}, z={(s-rs.mean())/rs.std():+.1f})\")\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3579,"cache_read_input_tokens":350614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3579},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c165a4c4-7f86-4a68-9c66-d8ef889e2fe6","uuid":"d6873b1d-6e23-4f18-816b-1dde40646f4c","timestamp":"2026-11-07T05:49:43.980Z","request_id":"req_011Cgo3StQc8GU4Lg2LEmH8j"}
